Single-generation disorder refers to a genetic disorder that appears in only one generation of a family and is not seen in previous generations.

Key points (facts):

It usually occurs due to a new (de novo) mutation in a gene.

The mutation arises in:

A parent’s germ cell (sperm or ovum), or

The early embryo after fertilization.

Parents typically do not show the disorder.

The affected individual may pass it to their children, depending on the type of mutation and inheritance pattern.

Common examples:

Achondroplasia

Duchenne muscular dystrophy (in some cases)

Neurofibromatosis type 1 (often de novo)

Important distinction:

Unlike inherited disorders that follow Mendelian patterns across generations, single-generation disorders start fresh due to mutation rather than inheritance
